From: dev-cuttlefish <noreply@junsun.net>
Subject: [PATCH] backend/grpc_client: drop unused file-scope ClientContext

src/backend/grpc_client.cc declares `grpc::ClientContext context_;` at
file scope but never reads or writes it -- the per-call contexts live in
`contexts_` (an std::unordered_map keyed by stream_id). The dead static
is still default-constructed at program startup, which on platforms
where libgrpc is statically linked (riscv64 cuttlefish-base .deb build)
runs grpc::ClientContext::ClientContext() before grpc's own static
initializer (_GLOBAL__sub_I_client_context.cc) has had a chance to set
grpc::g_client_callbacks. Result: SIGSEGV at +252 in ClientContext's
ctor while dereferencing a NULL g_client_callbacks.

On amd64/aarch64 AOSP builds this works because libgrpc++ is a shared
library; its .init_array runs during DSO load, before _start invokes
the application's own static initializers. With the static-linked
riscv64 build, both initializer sets are concatenated into one
.init_array and ordering between TUs from different libraries is
unspecified -- netsim's TU happens to land at position 12 while
grpc's lands at position 25.

The static is dead code; deleting it removes the dependency entirely.

(See also the alwayslink patch to grpc's bazel/grpc_build_system.bzl
which ensures grpc's static initializer is preserved in the link --
needed so that later (post-main) ClientContext constructions in the
same binary do not also crash on a NULL g_client_callbacks.)
---
 src/backend/grpc_client.cc | 1 -
 1 file changed, 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/backend/grpc_client.cc b/src/backend/grpc_client.cc
--- a/src/backend/grpc_client.cc
+++ b/src/backend/grpc_client.cc
@@ -53,7 +53,6 @@ std::unordered_map<uint32_t, grpc::ClientContext> contexts_;
 // Single connection to a server with multiple StreamPackets calls
 std::string server_;
 std::shared_ptr<grpc::Channel> channel_;
-grpc::ClientContext context_;
 std::unique_ptr<netsim::packet::PacketStreamer::Stub> stub_;

 // Call the StreamPackets RPC on server.
